Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Company Material Adverse Effect' means any event, change, condition, or effect (each, an 'Effect') that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that, for purposes of clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to be or constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: (a) changes in general economic conditions, (b) changes in the industries in which the Company operates, (c) changes in laws or regulations, (d) acts of war or terrorism, (e) natural disasters or pandemics, (f) changes in the market price of the Company's stock, (g) failure to meet financial projections, (h) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement, or (i) any action taken with the written consent of Parent. (Pages 15-16)\nAnswer:
Choices:

